/ {
    /*
     * Keyboard -> host: announce the active layers (rafaelromao/zmk-layer-hud).
     *
     * Not included: the announcement rides in the keyboard report, and the host
     * reads it as a key. Turn it back on once the signal has its own report.
     *
     * The premise was that 0xA5-0xDF are reserved and no OS maps them. Linux
     * does. Every unmapped slot in the kernel's hid_keyboard[] table holds the
     * macro `unk`, which is KEY_UNKNOWN (240), not zero -- so the "if unmapped,
     * ignore" branch never fires and the whole range arrives as keycode 240.
     * Measured with evtest on the Diamond:
     *
     *   MSC_SCAN 700d6 -> KEY_UNKNOWN (240)   NAV, 0xC0 + 22
     *   MSC_SCAN 700df -> KEY_UNKNOWN (240)   the commit usage
     *
     * So every layer change emits a phantom key press carrying whatever
     * modifiers are held. Holding Gui and touching the nav layer was enough to
     * make Hyprland change workspace, no digit involved. It also breaks host
     * auto-repeat, which the module's own comment already noted.
     *
     * There is no base-usage that dodges this -- the only keyboard-page usages
     * Linux really ignores are 0x00-0x03, the error and rollover codes.
     *
     * `positions` is separately unusable here: it costs two blocking
     * zmk_endpoint_send_report calls per key press on the system work queue,
     * which is the queue ZMK scans the matrix on. The next press is timestamped
     * late and combo.c measures the combo term from those timestamps; against
     * COMBO_TERM of 30 ms, combos stop firing. A dedicated queue does not fix
     * it while the report is shared with real keys -- two threads writing one
     * report race, which broke combos a second way. Its own report fixes both.
     */
    layer_signal {
        compatible = "zmk,layer-signal";
        heartbeat-ms = <2000>;
    };
};
